cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite the software release and the
  accompanying manuscript.
title: "Three-Body Regularized Atlas: Proof-Carrying Finite Planar Continuation"
type: software
version: 0.3.0-review
date-released: 2026-07-17
authors:
  - family-names: Spaulding
    given-names: Jeffrey
    affiliation: Independent Researcher
    email: jspaulding42@hotmail.com
repository-code: "https://github.com/jspaulding42/three-body-regularized-atlas"
license: BSD-3-Clause
abstract: >-
  A reproducible proof-carrying checker for a supplied finite planar
  three-body point-IVP continuation chain. The checker freshly replays
  ordinary bridges and repeated Levi-Civita passages for all three canonical
  binary pairs, propagates clock and local gauge ledgers, and returns either a
  fixed-time enclosure or a typed unresolved prefix, conditional on its
  stated arithmetic and analytic trusted kernel. It is not a complete
  certificate producer, an independently implemented verifier, or a general
  closed-form solution of the three-body problem.
keywords:
  - three-body problem
  - Levi-Civita regularization
  - validated numerics
  - computer-assisted proof
  - interval arithmetic
notes: >-
  Jeffrey Spaulding conceived, prompted, and directed the project and is the
  accountable human author. OpenAI GPT-5.5, GPT-5.5 Pro, and GPT-5.6 systems
  contributed substantially to mathematical exploration, software
  implementation, verification design, manuscript drafting, and critical
  revision. The AI systems are credited as tools and contributors, not as
  scholarly authors, and cannot accept responsibility for the work.
